    "content": "The Orion spacecraft is NASA's crew exploration vehicle for the Artemis program, designed to carry four astronauts on deep-space missions, including lunar orbit and future Mars missions. It is a 67-foot-tall, three-part capsule (launch abort, crew, and service modules) built to handle deep space radiation and high-speed atmospheric re-entry, with the first crewed flight (Artemis II) launching in 2026. Orion spacecraft This video gives a detailed look inside the Orion spacecraft:05:25VideoFromSpaceYouTube• Mar 31, 2026 05:25 Key Features and Capabilities: Capacity: Designed for four crew members but can hold more in emergency situations, offering more space than Apollo. Mission Duration: Capable of operating for up to 21 days with crew, or six months docked at the Lunar Gateway. Structure: Comprises the Crew Module (living space), European Service Module (power, propulsion, water, air), and Launch Abort System. Safety: Features a state-of-the-art heat shield for 25,000 mph re-entry speeds and a launch abort system that can pull the crew to safety in milliseconds. Propulsion: Utilizes solar array wings on the service module for power. Usage: Built for the Artemis program to support long-term lunar exploration and act as a foundation for human missions to Mars. Artemis program Development Status: Artemis I: Completed an uncrewed test flight in 2022, proving the spacecraft’s systems in deep space. Artemis II: Scheduled to launch the first crew on a lunar flyby in April 2026, marking the first time humans return to the Moon in over 50 years. Production: As of 2026, multiple Orion capsules are under construction or completed for subsequent missions (Artemis III, IV, etc.). Orion capsules",
